      "problem": "Factor the expression $x^3 - 6x^2 + 9x.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, notice that all of the terms have a common factor of $x.$ To simplify the computation, we can factor out the $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx^3 - 6x^2 + 9x = x(x^2 - 6x + 9)\n$$\n\nThe expression $x^2 - 6x + 9$ is a perfect square trinomial that can be factored as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x({x}^{2}-6x+9) & = \\\\ x({x}^{2}-6x+{3}^{2}) & = \\\\ x({x}^{2}-2\\cdot 3x+{3}^{2}) & = \\\\ x(x-3)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
